Q: add e-mail recipient into php script A: <p>Use the built in mailer methods for Joomla:</p>

<pre><code>$msg = "This is my email message.";
$subject = "Database Update Email";
$to = (string)$this-&gt;db-&gt;get('email');
$config = JFactory::getConfig();
$fromemail = $config-&gt;get('mailfrom');
$fromname = $config-&gt;get('fromname');
$from = array($fromemail,$fromname);

$mailer = JFactory::getMailer();
$mailer-&gt;setSender($from);
$mailer-&gt;addRecipient($to);
$mailer-&gt;addRecipient('you1@yourdomain.com');
$mailer-&gt;addRecipient('you2@yourdomain.com');
$mailer-&gt;addCC('you3@yourdomain.com');
$mailer-&gt;addBCC('you4@yourdomain.com');
$mailer-&gt;setSubject($subject);
$mailer-&gt;setBody($msg);
$mailer-&gt;isHTML();
$mailer-&gt;send();
</code></pre>

<p>This should use PHP to send an HTML email to whomever you want and copy other users on the email through either direct send, CC, or BCC depending on which method you use.</p>

Q: How to give one query result to another query in where condition using codeiginter? A: <p><strong><em>If you are using CI model functions than you have use this code:</em></strong></p>

<pre><code>    $this-&gt;db-&gt;select('*');
    $this-&gt;db-&gt;from('tipup_payment');        
    $this-&gt;db-&gt;where('TAcNo = select TRAcNo from tipup_receipt where TVAcNo = "'.$TAcNo.'"');
    $query = $this-&gt;db-&gt;get();
    return $query-&gt;result();
</code></pre>

Q: Limit visibility of a struct to a single file, yet use that struct in a public class A: <p>C# 11 introduced <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-gb/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/keywords/file" rel="nofollow noreferrer">file-local types</a>. At first glance, this sounds like exactly what you want, but there are restrictions, including:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A file-local type can't be a field member of a non-file-local type.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It looks like this also includes using it as a type argument for a field. So for example, this might <em>look</em> like it would work, but it doesn't, assuming you want <code>MyClass</code> itself to be a &quot;normal&quot; class:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>// Example that *doesn't* work.
namespace MyProjectNamespace
{
    // This is a file-local type...
    file struct MyHelperStruct
    {
        public int memberA;
        public bool memberB;
        public DateTime memberC;
        // ...
    }

    public class MyClass
    {
        // But that prohibits its use as a type argument here
        private List&lt;MyHelperStruct&gt; _myVars;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>If you only need to use it within <code>MyClass</code>, just declare it private nested within <code>MyClass</code>. It's then <em>only</em> available to <code>MyClass</code> (and if <code>MyClass</code> were nested inside another type, it would be available within that too). Note that this isn't about <em>files</em> - it's about the accessibility of each type with respect to other types. If you had another type as a peer in the same file as <code>MyClass</code>, then a private type nested within <code>MyClass</code> would <em>not</em> be visible in that other peer type.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>// Example that *does* work.
namespace MyProjectNamespace
{
    public class MyClass
    {
        private List&lt;MyHelperStruct&gt; _myVars;

        private struct MyHelperStruct
        {
            public int memberA;
            public bool memberB;
            public DateTime memberC;
            // ...
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>As a side-note, I'd strongly recommend <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/language-reference/keywords/namespace" rel="nofollow noreferrer">file-scoped namespaces</a> to avoid an extra level of unnecessary nesting in source code.</p>
